{"id": "cs-44", "input": {"messages": [{"sender": "Architect", "text": "Recap of the architecture discussion for anyone who missed it. The current message bus handles about four thousand events per second at peak, and we have seen consumer lag climb above ten minutes during month-end batch jobs. Three options were on the table: tune RabbitMQ with quorum queues, move to a managed cloud queue, or adopt Kafka with tiered storage. The team walked through cost, operational burden, replay needs for the audit service, and the skills we already have in house. Replay was the deciding factor because the audit team needs to re-read thirty days of events after schema changes. Managed queues were cheaper to run but do not give us long retention. Tuning RabbitMQ buys time but does not solve replay. Recap of the architecture discussion for anyone who missed it. The current message bus handles about four thousand events per second at peak, and we have seen consumer lag climb above ten minutes during month-end batch jobs. Three options were on the table: tune RabbitMQ with quorum queues, move to a managed cloud queue, or adopt Kafka with tiered storage. The team walked through cost, operational burden, replay needs for the audit service, and the skills we already have in house. Replay was the deciding factor because the audit team needs to re-read thirty days of events after schema changes. Managed queues were cheaper to run but do not give us long retention. Tuning RabbitMQ buys time but does not solve replay. So we agreed to replace RabbitMQ with Kafka by the end of Q2, starting with the billing topics."}, {"sender": "Dev", "text": "Thanks for the write-up."}]}, "expected": {}, "tags": ["edge", "long-message"], "expect_non_empty": ["summary"], "expect_contains": {"decisions": "kafka", "asks": "[]"}}
